<div class="doc">

<section class="panel" id="quick-start">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Quick start</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p>The first visit opens a setup wizard that creates the admin account and the initial theme. After that, sign in and upload from the dashboard.</p>
    <pre class="snippet"><span class="cmt"># local development</span>
npm install
cp .env.example .env
npm start            <span class="cmt"># http://localhost:3000</span>

<span class="cmt"># docker (single service, switches role with SERVER_MODE)</span>
docker compose up -d                                  <span class="cmt"># main server on :3000</span>
SERVER_MODE=node NODE_ACCESS_KEY=secret docker compose up -d   <span class="cmt"># storage node</span></pre>
    <p>Metadata lives in SQLite through Sequelize; file contents stay on disk in <span class="code">data/uploads</span> or on storage nodes.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="workspace">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Workspace editors</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p><span class="code">/create</span> opens the template gallery. Every editor saves automatically to the document record in SQLite and keeps a version history.</p>
    <table class="table" style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--r-md);overflow:hidden">
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What works today</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">Document</td><td class="muted">Rich text, headings, lists, quotes, alignment, links, images, tables, header/footer, word count, Word (.docx) export with embedded images.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">Spreadsheet</td><td class="muted">Formula engine with dependency tracking (SUM, AVERAGE, MIN, MAX, COUNT, PRODUCT, ROUND, ABS, IF), formatting, multiple sheets, column sort/filter, Excel (.xlsx) export.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">Presentation</td><td class="muted">Drag/resize text, shapes and images, layouts, real thumbnails, snapping and guides, slide transitions (fade/push/wipe/zoom), per-element entrance animations and click builds, presenter mode, speaker notes, undo/redo, PowerPoint (.pptx) export with native slide transitions, and editable import of uploaded .pptx files.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">Website</td><td class="muted">Visual page builder with sections, layers, drag reorder, desktop/tablet/phone previews, theme tokens, SEO/Open Graph, image library, reusable components, sandboxed custom HTML/CSS/JS, analytics, form responses, sitemap/robots, custom domains, and standalone HTML or React export.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">PDF</td><td class="muted">Real PDF rendering with zoom, page navigation and text selection; highlight selections, place text boxes, comments and drawn/uploaded signatures; rotate, reorder, duplicate, insert, delete, merge and split pages; export burns every annotation into a downloadable PDF. Starter templates: blank, ruled notes, cover, checklist.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">Whiteboard</td><td class="muted">Infinite pan/zoom canvas, pen, rectangles, ellipses, arrows, text, sticky notes, eraser, undo/redo, SVG export.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">Form</td><td class="muted">Visual builder (8 field types), publish link, server-side validation, response storage, per-question analytics, CSV export.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">Code</td><td class="muted">Multi-file project with tabs, syntax highlighting, Tab indentation, rename/delete, autosave and export of the active file.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">Discord bot (Flow)</td><td class="muted">Node-based Discord bot IDE: typed exec/data pins, branching loops and switches, a multi-script project explorer, Discord.js/AI/HTTP/database/Lavalink nodes, node-linked compiler diagnostics, graph simulation, and single-file or modular runnable exports.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">Roblox Studio</td><td class="muted">A real bridge, not a mockup: download the Luau plugin, pair a local Studio session with a one-time code, then browse and edit the live Explorer, properties, Luau scripts and GUI. The plugin applies every supported change; unsupported properties are reported instead of faked.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">Game Studio</td><td class="muted">Real 2D and 3D game projects. 2D uses Canvas2D + Matter.js; 3D uses Three.js + Cannon-es with a scene editor, hierarchy, transform gizmos, PBR materials, lighting/shadows, character controller, colliders, joints, particles, terrain and UI. Script gameplay with the visual graph compiler or sandboxed JavaScript, press Play to run the actual runtime, then export one self-contained HTML file, a ZIP with source and assets, or publish at <span class="code">/games/:slug</span>. Starters include first-person, third-person, physics sandbox, driving, survival and 2D platformer/top-down projects.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h3>Saving, versions and sharing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Autosave debounces 0.8s; <span class="code">Ctrl/Cmd + S</span> saves immediately and the status chip shows the result.</li>
      <li>Every save creates a snapshot; <strong>History</strong> in the editor bar lists the last 25 versions and can restore any of them.</li>
      <li>If another session saved first, the editor shows a conflict bar: <em>Load theirs</em> or <em>Keep mine</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Live collaboration</strong> syncs every editor over Yjs/WebSocket: remote edits, selections and cursors, usernames/colors, presence, live saves, reconnect recovery, and an offline draft that merges back. The server enforces roles and takes revoked access out of the room immediately.</li>
      <li><strong>Save as template</strong> stores the current content under <em>Create → Custom</em>. Template cards render the real template content in an authenticated iframe; clicking <strong>Preview</strong> opens the full-size render.</li>
      <li><strong>Share</strong> invites a username or email at Owner / Editor / Commenter / Viewer level, creates expiring capability links at <span class="code">/s/:token</span>, and publishes read-only pages at <span class="code">/c/:slug</span>. Threaded comments support replies, resolve/reopen and @mentions; version history can restore a previous snapshot.</li>
      <li><strong>Websites</strong> publish to <span class="code">/sites/:slug</span> with sitemap/robots, SEO tags, a CSP-scoped contact form stored in SQLite, view/form analytics and custom-domain Host routing. Uploaded images are served through the published site's own asset route; HTML/JSX exports inline them. The **CMS** panel creates SQLite-backed collections and items, renders dynamic `/c/:collection` and `/c/:collection/:slug` pages, and the **Deployments** panel keeps immutable staging/production snapshots with one-click rollback.</li>
      <li><strong>Image library</strong> in the document, spreadsheet and deck editors lists your own uploads, accepts new images, and searches free Wikimedia Commons images with author and license attribution. Spreadsheet images are exported as anchored XLSX drawings.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Current limits</h3>
    <p class="hint">Implemented deliberately without external editing services. PDF pages are rendered with pdf.js and rewritten with pdf-lib in the browser; annotations are applied server-side. Documents, sheets and decks export to native DOCX/XLSX/PPTX with embedded images. PowerPoint files can be imported: upload a <span class="mono">.pptx</span> and it opens as an editable presentation, or use <em>Edit as presentation</em> on an uploaded file. DOCX/XLSX <em>import</em> is not included. Custom website code runs in a sandboxed iframe with no same-origin access and no network; the published page CSP allows only that inline frame code, not arbitrary top-level scripts. Custom domains need TLS termination at your reverse proxy. AI assistance is optional and configured in <a href="/admin/ai">Admin → Cascade AI</a>.</p>
    <h3>Cascade Game Studio</h3>
    <p class="hint">Game files are workspace documents with <span class="code">kind=game</span> and <span class="code">mode=2d|3d</span>. Compile via <span class="code">POST /api/workspace/docs/:id/game/compile</span>, preview via <span class="code">GET …/game/preview</span>, export via <span class="code">POST …/game/export</span> (<span class="code">mode=html|zip</span>), and publish through the normal <span class="code">…/publish</span> route. The exported game inlines the engine, runtime, project and data-URL assets and has no Cascade dependency. 3D terrain is a procedural heightfield; navigation/behavior trees, skeletal animation playback and blending, networked 3D play, mesh editing/sculpting, and advanced post-processing are not implemented yet. Use Play mode, the Problems tab and the browser QA flow before calling a game finished.</p>
    <h3>Cascade Flow bots</h3>
    <p class="hint">Create a bot from <span class="code">/create</span> with the Development category or open a <span class="code">flow</span> document. Graphs compile to real async JavaScript; exports include <span class="code">package.json</span>, <span class="code">.env.example</span> and a README. Credentials are read only from environment variables and raw secret-shaped project fields are stripped from exports. Lavalink playback needs a reachable Lavalink server; AI nodes need an OpenAI-compatible endpoint and key; real Discord integration needs a bot token and a test guild. The simulator traces routing and payloads without connecting to Discord. Webhook Handler nodes generate a real HTTP listener: set <span class="code">FLOW_WEBHOOK_HOST</span>/<span class="code">FLOW_WEBHOOK_PORT</span>, expose it through your hosting router, and configure <span class="code">secretEnv</span> so requests need <span class="code">x-webhook-secret</span> or an HMAC <span class="code">x-flow-signature</span>. Custom JavaScript and Define Function nodes execute with the bot's server privileges, so review them before deploying. A custom node package can alias a supported built-in operation with a versioned <span class="code">custom:</span> type; it then appears in the Custom library category and compiles to that operation. Packages that alias unsafe code need the <span class="code">unsafe-code</span> permission; HTTP, database, AI and music aliases need <span class="code">network</span>, <span class="code">database</span>, <span class="code">ai</span> and <span class="code">voice</span> respectively, and packages that declare the wrong or no permission are rejected by the compiler.</p>
    <h3>Roblox Studio limits</h3>
    <p class="hint">The plugin is the final authority and needs a real Roblox Studio install with HTTP requests enabled. Terrain sculpting, animation timelines, particle simulation, pathfinding visualization and asset uploads are not exposed through the plugin; the browser labels those workflows instead of pretending to apply them. The Three.js viewport is an approximation (MeshParts render as placeholders) and uses HTTP polling because Roblox plugins cannot depend on WebSockets. Use <span class="code">docs/roblox-studio-manual-checklist.md</span> for the full manual acceptance pass.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="roblox-studio">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Roblox Studio bridge</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p>Open <span class="code">/studio/roblox</span>, create a place, then choose <strong>Connect Studio</strong>. The one-time pairing URL and code are handed to the local plugin; the plugin exchanges them for a hashed bearer session and polls the relay for supported commands. No Roblox password, cookie or API token is ever requested.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The plugin is built from <span class="code">plugin/CascadePlugin.luau</span> and served as an installable <span class="code">.rbxmx</span> at <span class="code">/studio/roblox/plugin.rbxmx</span>.</li>
      <li>Sessions heartbeat every ten seconds, become <em>stale</em> when polls stop, and can be revoked by an editor immediately.</li>
      <li>Browser edits are validated against a scene schema (8 MB, 8,000 nodes), queued as commands, and persisted optimistically so a failed plugin connection cannot lose the work.</li>
      <li>Scene snapshots are real document versions; restore a snapshot in the browser and the next plugin snapshot reconciles Studio.</li>
      <li>Live collaboration uses the same Yjs room as the rest of the workspace; viewers watch, editors publish, and presence/cursors appear in the top bar.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Authorized MCP agents use the Studio tools listed below. They can inspect the hierarchy and queue validated batches, but cannot execute arbitrary Luau through the relay.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="ai">
  <div class="panel-head">
    <div>
      <div class="panel-title">Cascade AI</div>
      <div class="panel-sub">A native AI workspace at <span class="code">/ai</span> — not an embedded third-party UI.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p><span class="code">/ai</span> streams answers over SSE, renders Markdown, KaTeX and highlighted code, and keeps a branching conversation tree. The sidebar supports folders, search, pinning and deleting conversations. Messages can be regenerated; sibling branches are switchable with the arrow controls. Attachments are uploaded through the normal Cascade file store: images are passed to vision models and text/PDF/Office documents are extracted server-side for analysis. Knowledge bases chunk, embed and retrieve documents, and an assistant can pin its own system prompt, model and knowledge bases.</p>
    <p>Provider credentials are stored server-side in settings (or read from <span class="code">apiKeyEnv</span>) and are never included in <span class="code">/api/ai/*</span> responses. The browser only receives provider names, model IDs and a <span class="code">hasKey</span> flag. Supported provider types are <strong>OpenAI-compatible</strong> (<span class="code">/chat/completions</span>, <span class="code">/models</span>) and <strong>Ollama</strong> (<span class="code">/api/chat</span>, <span class="code">/api/tags</span>). Image generation/editing, speech synthesis, web search (SearXNG/Tavily) and embeddings are configured independently; without an embedding provider, knowledge retrieval falls back to a deterministic local lexical embedding.</p>
    <p><strong>Tools.</strong> Every conversation can call the same owner-scoped tools exposed at <span class="code">/mcp</span> — documents, decks, sheets, PDFs, boards, forms, code, websites, games, Flow projects, Roblox Studio and Cascade Motion — plus <span class="code">cascade_generate_image</span> for image generation. Generated artifacts are written to Cascade Files and the chat card links straight to <span class="code">/edit/:id</span>. External MCP servers can be added in Admin → Cascade AI with an optional bearer token; their tools are namespaced. Destructive tools (<span class="code">delete_document</span>, <span class="code">studio_queue_command</span>) pause the turn for an explicit Approve/Decline card and are never executed by the model on its own.</p>
    <p><strong>Configuration.</strong> Admin → Cascade AI holds providers and models, the default assistant prompt, image/speech/search/embedding endpoints, MCP servers, per-user daily token limits and tool-iteration limits. Provider model discovery runs against the saved key; the JSON editor keeps an existing key when <span class="code">apiKey</span> is blank.</p>
    <p><strong>Endpoints.</strong> <span class="code">POST /api/ai/chat</span> streams SSE (events: <span class="code">meta</span>, <span class="code">user</span>, <span class="code">assistant_start</span>, <span class="code">delta</span>, <span class="code">tool_start</span>, <span class="code">tool_result</span>, <span class="code">confirmation_required</span>, <span class="code">usage</span>, <span class="code">done</span>, <span class="code">error</span>); <span class="code">POST /api/ai/tool-confirm</span> continues a paused turn. Conversation CRUD, branching, assistants, knowledge bases, images, speech, web search and usage live under <span class="code">/api/ai/*</span> and require a signed-in session. Provider keys are never accepted from the browser.</p>
    <p class="hint">There is no built-in hosted model: until an administrator configures a provider with a working key (or a reachable Ollama instance), <span class="code">/ai</span> opens normally and chat replies with a clear “no provider configured” error instead of pretending to answer.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="auth">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Authentication</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p>Two ways in:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Session cookie</strong> — the browser login form. Use this for the web UI.</li>
      <li><strong>API key</strong> — for scripts and integrations. Create one in <a href="/admin/api-keys">Admin → API keys</a>; the full key is shown once. Choose <strong>read-only</strong>, <strong>read/write</strong> or <strong>full-owner</strong> scope.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Send the key in either header:</p>
    <pre class="snippet">x-api-key: csk_XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX

<span class="cmt"># or</span>
Authorization: Bearer csk_XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX</pre>
    <p>Only a SHA-256 hash of each key is stored. Revoking a key takes effect immediately (the next request gets <span class="code">401</span>). Read-only keys can read their owner's files and workspace but cannot mutate anything; read/write keys can mutate but cannot open admin pages; full-owner keys act as the owner, including admin access for admin-owned keys. A key never sees another account's private resources.</p>
    <p><strong>Registration</strong> is disabled by default. An administrator can enable it in <a href="/admin/users">Admin → Users</a>; the public form is at <span class="code">/register</span>. Check availability with <span class="code">GET /api/auth/config</span>, then create a standard (never admin) account with <span class="code">POST /api/auth/register</span>:</p>
    <pre class="snippet">curl -s http://localhost:3000/api/auth/register \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d '{"username":"alice","password":"at-least-6-chars"}'</pre>
    <p>Signed-in people can update their username, password and theme from <span class="code">/settings</span>; <span class="code">POST /settings/profile</span> and <span class="code">POST /settings/password</span> back those forms.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="mcp">
  <div class="panel-head">
    <div>
      <div class="panel-title">MCP server</div>
      <div class="panel-sub">Remote workspace access for MCP-compatible agents, authenticated with a Cascade API key.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p>Cascade exposes a stateless <strong>Streamable HTTP</strong> MCP endpoint at <span class="code">/mcp</span>. It uses the same API keys and owner checks as the rest of the app: a key can only list and read its owner's workspace, and a read-only key cannot call mutation tools.</p>
    <p>Available tools: <span class="code">whoami</span>, <span class="code">list_documents</span>, <span class="code">get_document</span>, <span class="code">list_versions</span>, <span class="code">get_version</span>, <span class="code">list_folders</span>, <span class="code">list_templates</span>, <span class="code">search_documents</span>, <span class="code">list_form_responses</span>, <span class="code">create_document</span>, <span class="code">update_document</span>, <span class="code">update_document_metadata</span> (rename, star, move, share), <span class="code">create_folder</span>, <span class="code">restore_version</span>, <span class="code">duplicate_document</span>, and <span class="code">delete_document</span> (requires <span class="code">confirm: true</span>). Roblox tools: <span class="code">studio_list_projects</span>, <span class="code">studio_get_scene</span>, <span class="code">studio_list_commands</span>, <span class="code">studio_queue_command</span> and <span class="code">studio_create_snapshot</span>; mutations support a preview response when <span class="code">dry_run</span> is set. Flow tools: <span class="code">flow_list_templates</span>, <span class="code">flow_get_project</span>, <span class="code">flow_compile</span> (optional <span class="code">include_code</span>), <span class="code">flow_export</span> (single or modular, optional <span class="code">include_files</span>) and <span class="code">flow_create_script</span>; all use the same owner and read-only scope checks. Documents are also exposed as <span class="code">cascade://documents/:id</span> resources for clients that support resources. See <span class="code">docs/mcp-server.md</span> for the full workflow.</p>
    <pre class="snippet">KEY=csk_XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX
URL=http://localhost:3000/mcp

<span class="cmt"># initialize (JSON-RPC; the endpoint is stateless)</span>
curl -s "$URL" \
  -H "x-api-key: $KEY" -H "content-type: application/json" \
  -H "accept: application/json, text/event-stream" \
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2025-06-18"}}'

<span class="cmt"># list and read tools</span>
curl -s "$URL" -H "x-api-key: $KEY" -H "content-type: application/json" \
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/list"}'

curl -s "$URL" -H "x-api-key: $KEY" -H "content-type: application/json" \
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":3,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"get_document","arguments":{"id":"DOC_ID"}}}'</pre>
    <p>The full plaintext key is shown only when it is created. Use it in the <span class="code">x-api-key</span> (or <span class="code">Authorization: Bearer</span>) header, never in the URL. Missing or revoked keys get <span class="code">401</span>; cookie-authenticated cross-origin MCP requests are rejected with <span class="code">403</span>. GET is not used (<span class="code">405</span>); POST every JSON-RPC message.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="api">
  <div class="panel-head">
    <div>
      <div class="panel-title">API reference</div>
      <div class="panel-sub">All endpoints accept a session cookie or an API key unless marked public.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <table class="table" style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--r-md);overflow:hidden">
      <thead><tr><th>Endpoint</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><span class="method method-get">GET</span> <span class="mono">/api/health</span></td>
          <td class="muted">Public. Server mode, setup state and node status.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><span class="method method-get">GET</span> <span class="mono">/api/me</span></td>
          <td class="muted">Current account, storage usage and which auth was used.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><span class="method method-get">GET</span> <span class="mono">/api/files</span></td>
          <td class="muted">List files. <span class="code">?limit=100</span>, admins can add <span class="code">?all=1</span>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><span class="method method-get">GET</span> <span class="mono">/api/files/:id</span></td>
          <td class="muted">Single file with raw and public URLs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><span class="method method-get">GET</span> <span class="mono">/api/images/search</span></td>
          <td class="muted">Editor image library. Keyless Wikimedia Commons search via <span class="code">?q=</span>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><span class="method method-get">GET</span> <span class="mono">/api/images/proxy</span></td>
          <td class="muted">Authenticated, Wikimedia-host-only image proxy used by search results.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><span class="method method-post">POST</span> <span class="mono">/api/upload</span></td>
          <td class="muted">Multipart upload, field name <span class="code">files</span>, up to 20 files per request.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><span class="method method-get">GET</span> <span class="mono">/api/files/:id/text</span></td>
          <td class="muted">Read a text file (2 MB max) for editing.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><span class="method method-put">PUT</span> <span class="mono">/api/files/:id/text</span></td>
          <td class="muted">Rewrite a text file to every replica. Body: <span class="code">{ "text": "…" }</span>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><span class="method method-post">POST</span> <span class="mono">/api/files/:id/share</span></td>
          <td class="muted">Toggle public sharing. Body: <span class="code">{ "isPublic": true }</span>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><span class="method method-post">POST</span> <span class="mono">/api/files/:id/rename</span></td>
          <td class="muted">Rename, and re-detect the MIME type. Body: <span class="code">{ "filename": "new.txt" }</span>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><span class="method method-delete">DELETE</span> <span class="mono">/api/files/:id</span></td>
          <td class="muted">Delete the file from every replica and the database.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><span class="method method-get">GET</span> <span class="mono">/f/:id/raw</span></td>
          <td class="muted">Stream a private file (owner or admin).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><span class="method method-get">GET</span> <span class="mono">/raw/:slug</span></td>
          <td class="muted">Public CDN URL for a shared file, 5-minute revalidating public cache.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Upload and share a file</h3>
    <pre class="snippet">KEY=csk_XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX

<span class="cmt"># upload</span>
curl -H "x-api-key: $KEY" -F "files=@./report.pdf" http://localhost:3000/api/upload

<span class="cmt"># list files</span>
curl -H "x-api-key: $KEY" http://localhost:3000/api/files

<span class="cmt"># make it public, then fetch the CDN URL</span>
curl -H "x-api-key: $KEY" -H "content-type: application/json" \
  -d '{"isPublic":true}' http://localhost:3000/api/files/FILE_ID/share
curl http://localhost:3000/raw/SLUG</pre>
    <p>Uploads return one result per file with its <span class="code">id</span>, share <span class="code">slug</span> and the replicas that received a copy. A quota failure is reported per file, so a batch can partially succeed.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="sharing">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Sharing &amp; CDN</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <ul>
      <li><span class="code">/c/:slug</span> — a share page with preview and download button.</li>
      <li><span class="code">/raw/:slug</span> — the raw bytes with <span class="code">Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, must-revalidate</span>. Embed it in an <span class="code">&lt;img&gt;</span>, a video tag or behind any CDN; revocation takes effect at the origin and cached copies are short-lived.</li>
      <li>Turning sharing off invalidates both URLs immediately (they return 404).</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="nodes">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Nodes &amp; replication</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p>A node is the same image with <span class="code">SERVER_MODE=node</span>. The main server stores locally too and counts as the <span class="code">local</span> node.</p>
    <pre class="snippet"><span class="cmt"># node protocol — the main server sends x-node-key on every call</span>
GET    /node/health
GET    /node/stat
PUT    /node/store/:id     <span class="cmt"># raw bytes, x-filename + content-type headers</span>
GET    /node/file/:id
DELETE /node/file/:id</pre>
    <p>Replication modes (Admin → Nodes):</p>
    <table class="table" style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--r-md);overflow:hidden">
      <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">all</td><td class="muted">Every enabled node gets a copy; new nodes are topped up by the health loop.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">select</td><td class="muted">Only the nodes ticked in the form.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">single</td><td class="muted">Exactly one copy, preferring local.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">default</td><td class="muted">Always the chosen node.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">n</td><td class="muted">Exactly N copies; re-replicates onto a healthy node when one drops.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="hint">Health checks run every <span class="code">HEALTHCHECK_INTERVAL_MS</span> (30s by default). Reads fall back across replicas, primary first.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="env">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Configuration</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <table class="table" style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--r-md);overflow:hidden">
      <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Default</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">PORT</td><td class="mono tiny">3000</td><td class="muted">HTTP port.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">HOST</td><td class="mono tiny">0.0.0.0</td><td class="muted">Bind address.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">SERVER_MODE</td><td class="mono tiny">main</td><td class="muted">main server or storage node.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">SESSION_SECRET</td><td class="mono tiny">random per process</td><td class="muted">Cookie signing secret. Set a strong value in production; without it sessions do not survive a restart.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">DB_PATH</td><td class="mono tiny">./data/cascade.sqlite</td><td class="muted">SQLite file used by Sequelize.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">STORAGE_DIR</td><td class="mono tiny">./data/uploads</td><td class="muted">Local blob directory.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">NODE_ACCESS_KEY</td><td class="mono tiny">generated</td><td class="muted">Node mode key; auto-generated to <span class="code">data/node-key.txt</span> if unset.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">HEALTHCHECK_INTERVAL_MS</td><td class="mono tiny">30000</td><td class="muted">Node health + replication heal interval.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono tiny">SITE_NAME, THEME_*</td><td class="mono tiny">Cascade</td><td class="muted">Initial branding; editable later in Admin → Appearance.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="storage">
  <div class="panel-head"><div class="panel-title">Storage &amp; backups</div></div>
  <div class="panel-body doc">
    <p>Everything persistent is under <span class="code">data/</span>:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="code">cascade.sqlite</span> — users, files, nodes, settings, API keys and analytics events.</li>
      <li><span class="code">uploads/</span> — local blobs, named by file id.</li>
      <li><span class="code">node-key.txt</span> — node mode access key, when auto-generated.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Back up both the database file and the uploads directory. On Docker, both live in the <span class="code">cascade-data</span> volume.</p>
    <pre class="snippet"><span class="cmt"># copy the SQLite database out of a running container</span>
docker compose exec cascade node -e "require('node:fs').copyFileSync('data/cascade.sqlite','data/backup.sqlite')"
docker compose cp cascade:/app/data/cascade.sqlite ./cascade.sqlite</pre>
    <p class="hint">An older <span class="code">data/db.json</span> store is imported automatically the first time Cascade starts with an empty database.</p>
  </div>
</section>

</div>
<%- include('partials/foot') %>
